{
  "_note": "The recordings half of the golden suite (§5.7). One row per case in transcripts.json; the words to say are that case's `transcript`, so a clip and its expectation can never drift apart. This file says what to record, how loud, how long, and how wrong a transcript of it may be; audio/README.md says what to call the file and where to put it. No audio is checked into this repository — see audio/README.md for that decision and how to reverse it.",

  "version": 1,

  "_format": "What `make journal-audio-check` requires of a clip. 16 kHz mono is what both transcribers want: Whisper resamples to it and Gemma 4's audio encoder is documented at it, so recording anything else means a resample somebody has to get right. scripts/journal-eval/prepare-audio.sh converts a phone recording to this in one pass.",
  "format": {
    "container": "wav",
    "codec": "pcm_s16le",
    "sample_rate": 16000,
    "channels": 1,
    "max_seconds": 60,
    "also_accepted": ["m4a", "mp3", "ogg", "opus", "flac"]
  },

  "_conditions": "Every case is recorded twice. `clean` is the take; `noisy` is the same words under the condition §12.1 calls certain for café check-ins — either recorded again in a real noisy room, or derived from the clean take with prepare-audio.sh --noise, which mixes a bed at a stated SNR and is reproducible from a seed. Which of the two was used is recorded per clip in audio/manifest.lock.json, because a derived noisy clip and a real one are not the same evidence.",
  "conditions": {
    "clean": { "label": "clean", "how": "A quiet room, 20-30 cm from the microphone, no music, no other voices.", "target_snr_db": null },
    "noisy": { "label": "noisy", "how": "A café, a tram, or a clean take with a noise bed mixed in at the stated SNR.", "target_snr_db": 10 }
  },

  "_difficulty": "A per-clip WER ceiling, stated as a class rather than as 240 separate numbers, so that a reviewer can see *why* a clip is allowed the error it is allowed. `plain` is the default. `hard` carries a proper name, a place name, an abbreviation, numerals or a borrowed word — tokens no language model can guess from context. `disfluent` is filler, speed or a sixty-word run-on. `low-level` is deliberately quiet or deliberately loud, where the microphone's own behaviour is part of the test. `short` is a clip of two or three words, where WER is quantised to steps of 0.33 or 0.5 and a rate is the wrong way to read it: 0.5 there means *at most one word wrong*, not *half the words*. These numbers are a first draft to be revised by the first run against a real model, in the report and with the reasoning (§5.7).",
  "difficulty": {
    "plain": { "clean": 0.15, "noisy": 0.3 },
    "hard": { "clean": 0.25, "noisy": 0.45 },
    "disfluent": { "clean": 0.3, "noisy": 0.5 },
    "low-level": { "clean": 0.25, "noisy": 0.45 },
    "short": { "clean": 0.5, "noisy": 0.5 }
  },

  "_clips": "`case` is the id in transcripts.json. `seconds_hint` is what the sentence takes at an ordinary reading pace — a guide for the person recording, not a check. `words` is the reference token count, which is the denominator of that clip's WER. A clip row may carry its own `wer_ceiling` object to override its class; none does yet, and any that does should say why in a `note`.",
